<PageLayout title="Editorial policy" description="How we choose, check, label and correct what we publish." intro="How we choose, check, label and correct what we publish.">
  <h2>Evidence first</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Important factual claims are checked against a reliable source, preferably the original paper, official documentation or an authoritative institution.</li>
    <li>References are listed on every article so readers can inspect them.</li>
    <li>We never invent papers, authors, DOIs, statistics, quotes or findings.</li>
    <li>We never present a hypothesis as a discovery. Articles distinguish established evidence, interpretation, speculation and open questions.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Review</h2>
  <p>Every article is reviewed by an editor before publication. This is <strong>editorial review, not peer review</strong>. Where an article discusses peer-reviewed research, it says so and links to it.</p>
  <h2 id="ai">Use of AI tools</h2>
  <p>We may use AI tools to brainstorm, outline, edit or check clarity. A named person is responsible for every published article, and every factual claim is verified by a person. When AI assistance has materially shaped an article, the article says so. AI-assisted text is never described as peer reviewed.</p>
  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>When we get something wrong, we fix it and add a dated note to the article explaining what changed. To report an error, use the <a href="/contact/#corrections">corrections form</a> or write to <ContactEmail subject="Correction" />.</p>
  <h2>Images and copyright</h2>
  <p>We use images we have the right to use, credit them, and add descriptive alternative text. Where no suitable image exists, we use original illustrations. We quote sources briefly and with attribution.</p>
  <h2>Conflicts of interest</h2>
  <p>Authors disclose any relationship with the research, companies or people they write about. Disclosures appear on the article.</p>
</PageLayout>
